"""Focused programming and algorithm walkthroughs for concepts not exercised by existing examples."""
from . import T

T('3-c-memory', 'c-control-flow', 'Programming in C', 'C control flow: trace the loop and each branch', r'''
A C program executes statements according to its control flow, while expressions compute values used by those statements. A for loop first runs its initialization, then tests its condition before each iteration. After the body, its update expression runs and the condition is tested again. A false initial condition means the body never runs. Variables declared in the loop initializer have that loop's scope.
An if statement chooses its branch from a condition: zero is false and a nonzero scalar value is true. The remainder operator on positive integers is useful for parity tests. A continue in a for loop skips the rest of the current body but still proceeds to the loop update; break instead exits the loop. Confusing these two changes both the trace and the result.
Build a trace table containing the loop variable, condition result, selected branch and persistent accumulator. Update only statements that actually execute. Count the final failed condition separately from successful iterations. For a well-defined C question, derive the output from language rules rather than from one compiler run, and check that all reads use initialized objects.
''', 'Trace: int total=0; for (int i=1; i<=4; ++i) { if (i%2==0) continue; total+=i; } printf("%d",total); Assume printf is declared.', r'Initialize and visit one::i=1,\quad total=0+1=1::The condition is true and the odd branch reaches the addition.~Skip even iterations::i=2\ \text{and}\ i=4::The remainder is zero, so continue skips addition but does not skip ++i.~Visit three::total=1+3=4::The next odd iteration adds three to the value retained from the first iteration.~Terminate and print::i=5>4::The failed condition exits the loop; the printed accumulator is four.', 'The program prints 4.', 'The executed additions are exactly 1 and 3. Counting two successful additions separately from four loop iterations confirms the result.')

T('3-linear-structures', 'array-insertion', 'Arrays', 'Array insertion: capacity, indices and safe shifts', r'''
An array stores equal-sized elements in consecutive locations, so its index identifies an offset from the base address. Direct access to an existing element is constant time when the machine supports that addressing. The allocated capacity and the number of logically stored elements are separate quantities; a partly filled array can contain unused slots without those slots being members of the sequence.
Insertion at position p must preserve the order of the old suffix. First check that capacity exceeds logical length and that p is between zero and length inclusive. Shift existing elements from the end toward p, working right to left, and then write the new element. Shifting left to right overwrites values before they have been copied. Increase logical length only after constructing the expanded sequence.
Insertion near the front can move a linear number of elements even though each individual indexed read or write is constant time. Appending into available capacity moves none. Deletion uses the opposite direction to close a gap. Verify bounds, preservation of every original item, the new length and the insertion position rather than treating an array's fast indexing as proof that all its operations are constant time.
''', 'An array has capacity 5, logical length 4 and values [2,4,6,8]. Insert 5 at zero-based position 2.', r'Check room::4<5::There is one free slot and the requested position belongs to the valid insertion range.~Shift from the end::a[4]\leftarrow8,\quad a[3]\leftarrow6::Copy the old final element first, then its predecessor, so neither is overwritten.~Fill the gap::a[2]\leftarrow5,\quad length\leftarrow5::The insertion position is now free and the logical sequence expands by one.', 'The logical array becomes [2,4,5,6,8], with two shifted elements.', 'All four original values remain in their original relative order, and no index outside allocated slots 0 through 4 was accessed.')

T('3-trees-bst', 'tree-height-shape', 'Trees', 'Tree height, leaves and complete-tree shape', r'''
A rooted tree is a connected acyclic structure with a distinguished root. Each nonroot vertex has one parent, so a tree with n vertices has n−1 edges. A vertex's depth is the number of edges from the root to that vertex. Its height is the maximum number of edges from it to a descendant leaf. Under this convention a leaf has height zero and the tree's height is the root's height; some questions instead count vertices, so state the convention.
A binary tree permits at most two children per vertex, including positions distinguished as left and right. A full binary tree gives every vertex either zero or two children. A complete binary tree fills all levels except possibly the final level, whose occupied positions must form a leftmost prefix. A perfect tree fills every level. These definitions constrain different properties.
Compute height bottom up from child heights, and compute depth top down from the root. For shape questions, identify missing child positions rather than only counting vertices on a level. A right child with a missing left child violates completeness. Traversal order does not change any of these structural quantities.
''', 'A has children B,C; B has children D,E; C has only a right child F; F has only a left child G. Find vertex count, leaves, height and whether the tree is complete.', r'Count structure::n=7,\quad |E|=7-1=6::Each of B,C,D,E,F,G has one parent; the childless vertices are D,E,G.~Follow the deepest path::A\to C\to F\to G::This path has three edges, so G has depth three and the root has height three.~Check occupied positions::::At depth two, C has no left child but has right child F. Occupied positions therefore do not form a leftmost prefix.', 'There are 7 vertices, 6 edges and leaves D,E,G. The height is 3 edges; the binary tree is not complete.', 'Bottom-up heights are D=E=G=0, B=F=1, C=2 and A=3. C and F also show that the tree is not full.')

T('3-heaps-graphs', 'heap-bottom-up-build', 'Binary heaps', 'Bottom-up heap construction and its linear work bound', r'''
A binary min-heap combines a complete binary-tree shape with a local ordering rule: every parent is at most each of its children. Its zero-based array children are 2i+1 and 2i+2. Bottom-up construction begins at floor(n/2)−1, the last internal vertex, and sifts each internal vertex downward while visiting indices in decreasing order. Leaves already satisfy heap order.
A sift compares available children, chooses the smaller one and exchanges it with an oversized parent. Continue from that child's position until heap order holds or a leaf is reached. Choosing an arbitrary child can leave the other child smaller than its parent. Previously repaired child subtrees are essential: they make a local sift sufficient for repairing the current subtree.
The total work is linear because most vertices have small height. The number of vertices able to descend at least h levels is at most floor(n/2^h); summing these possible descents over h gives less than n. Constant comparisons per descended level and one initial check per internal vertex preserve O(n). Repeatedly inserting all elements is a different procedure with an O(n log n) worst-case bound.
''', 'Build a min-heap bottom up from [9,4,7,1,6,3,2], using zero-based indices and the smaller child for each sift.', r'Start at internal index two::\lfloor7/2\rfloor-1=2::Its children contain 3 and 2; exchange 7 with 2 to obtain [9,4,2,1,6,3,7].~Repair index one::::Its children are 1 and 6. Exchange 4 with 1 to obtain [9,1,2,4,6,3,7].~Repair the root::::Exchange 9 with child 1, then exchange that 9 with child 4. The array becomes [1,4,2,9,6,3,7].~Explain total work::\sum_{h\ge1}\lfloor n/2^h\rfloor<n::At most this many downward levels are available across all vertices; repeatedly charging every vertex a full log n height is an overestimate.', 'The min-heap is [1,4,2,9,6,3,7]. This construction used four downward exchanges and has O(n) total work in general.', 'The parent checks are 1≤4,2; 4≤9,6; and 2≤3,7. The array still contains exactly the original seven values and retains the complete shape.')

T('3-heaps-graphs', 'directed-graph-degrees', 'Graphs', 'Directed graphs: vertices, arcs and degree checks', r'''
A graph models vertices and connections. An undirected edge connects two endpoints without an orientation, while a directed arc has a source and a destination. For a directed graph, indegree counts incoming arcs and outdegree counts outgoing arcs. A directed self-loop contributes one to each of those counts. Distinguish a simple-graph convention that forbids self-loops from a representation that allows them.
Reachability follows permitted arc directions; an arc from A to B does not establish a reverse route. A directed cycle is a closed route that follows directions. Weak connectivity ignores directions, whereas strong connectivity requires mutual directed reachability. These properties are separate from vertex degree and from the physical way the graph is stored.
To build a graph from an edge list, first include the specified vertex set, including isolated vertices, then process each arc once. Increase its source's outdegree and its destination's indegree. Check the totals: both the sum of outdegrees and the sum of indegrees must equal the number of arcs. Degree totals verify counting but do not prove that the whole graph is connected.
''', 'Vertices are A,B,C,D. Directed arcs are A→B, A→C, C→B and B→B; self-loops are allowed. Find indegrees, outdegrees and vertices reachable from A.', r'Count outgoing arcs::out(A)=2,\ out(B)=1,\ out(C)=1,\ out(D)=0::The loop at B has B as its source exactly once.~Count incoming arcs::in(A)=0,\ in(B)=3,\ in(C)=1,\ in(D)=0::B receives arcs from A and C plus its own loop.~Follow directions::::A reaches B and C. Following their arcs adds no new vertex; D is isolated.~Check totals::\sum out=\sum in=4::Each of the four arcs contributes once to each total.', 'Indegrees are (0,3,1,0), outdegrees are (2,1,1,0), and the vertices reachable from A are A,B,C.', 'The sums agree with four arcs. D remains in the graph even though it contributes no adjacency entries and cannot be reached from A.')

T('3-heaps-graphs', 'adjacency-list-build', 'Adjacency list', 'Build and traverse a directed adjacency list', r'''
An adjacency list gives each vertex a collection of its outgoing neighbors. A directed arc u→v appears in u's list; it need not appear in v's list. An undirected edge normally produces two entries, one at each endpoint. Store a list header even for an isolated vertex, whose neighbor collection is empty. Weighted graphs require a weight alongside each neighbor, and parallel-edge conventions determine whether repeated entries are allowed.
An edge list can be converted by starting with all lists empty and appending each destination to its source list. Preserve or sort neighbor order according to the stated traversal rule, because exact BFS or DFS discovery order can depend on it. The representation does not require vertices' labels to be numeric array indices; a separate mapping can connect labels to storage.
Enumerating u's neighbors costs proportional to its outdegree, and enumerating all lists costs Θ(V+E), including the empty headers. Testing whether a particular arc exists may require scanning u's list unless an additional lookup structure is maintained. Do not treat adjacency lists as giving the same constant-time membership test as a direct-access matrix.
''', 'Build outgoing adjacency lists in the supplied order for vertices A,B,C,D and arcs A→C, C→B, A→B. Then enumerate A\'s neighbors and test B→A.', r'Create every header::::Initialize A=[], B=[], C=[], D=[] so the isolated vertex is represented.~Append by source::::A→C and A→B give A=[C,B]; C→B gives C=[B]. The other lists stay empty.~Perform requested operations::::Enumerating A visits C then B. Testing B→A scans B\'s empty outgoing list and reports absence.', 'A=[C,B], B=[], C=[B], D=[]. A has two outgoing neighbors; B→A is absent.', 'There are four headers and three neighbor entries, matching V+E storage. The presence of A→B does not imply the reverse directed arc.')

T('3-heaps-graphs', 'adjacency-matrix-build', 'Adjacency matrix', 'Build an adjacency matrix and interpret its rows', r'''
An adjacency matrix reserves one entry for each ordered pair of vertices. Fix a row and column order first. In an unweighted directed graph, set M[u,v]=1 when u→v exists and zero otherwise. An undirected matrix is symmetric because each edge is usable in both directions; directed input has no such requirement. A permitted self-loop appears on the diagonal.
A row describes outgoing arcs from its vertex, while a column describes incoming arcs to its vertex. Row and column sums therefore give outdegree and indegree for a binary directed matrix without parallel arcs. A matrix for a multigraph may store counts instead, and a weighted matrix needs a no-edge marker that cannot be confused with a valid zero-weight edge.
Matrix storage is Θ(V²) regardless of edge count. A specified arc can be checked in constant time once vertex indices are known, but finding all neighbors of one vertex requires inspecting its whole row. Traversing all rows costs Θ(V²), even when most entries are zero. Verify representation choices and vertex order before reading degrees or tracing a graph algorithm.
''', 'Use row/column order A,B,C for directed arcs A→B, B→C and C→B, without self-loops. Construct the matrix and find B\'s indegree and outdegree.', r'Initialize absent pairs::M=\begin{pmatrix}0&0&0\\0&0&0\\0&0&0\end{pmatrix}::All nine ordered pairs start absent, including diagonal pairs.~Set the directed entries::M=\begin{pmatrix}0&1&0\\0&0&1\\0&1&0\end{pmatrix}::Each supplied arc changes its source row and destination column only.~Read B\'s row and column::out(B)=1,\quad in(B)=2::Row B has the arc to C; column B receives arcs from A and C.', 'The rows are [0,1,0], [0,0,1], [0,1,0]. B has indegree 2 and outdegree 1.', 'The three ones match the three arcs. M[A,B]=1 but M[B,A]=0, demonstrating why a directed matrix need not be symmetric.')

T('4-analysis', 'worst-case-input', 'Asymptotic worst case time', 'Derive a worst-case function from actual inputs', r'''
Worst-case running time is the maximum work performed among all legal inputs of a fixed size. First define the measured operation and the input size, then derive that maximum. Big O describes an upper growth bound on the chosen function; it does not by itself say that the function is worst case. Best-case and average-case running times can also receive Big O bounds.
For a sequential search that stops at its first match, input order and target presence affect the comparison count. A target in the first position needs one equality test. A target only at the last position or absent altogether forces all n positions to be tested. Those inputs achieve the upper bound, so they justify a tight worst-case result rather than only a possible estimate.
When analyzing nested loops, recursion or graph algorithms, exhibit the pattern that makes the work large and prove that no legal input requires more. Do not infer an average by averaging the best and worst endpoints; an average needs a specified distribution. Exact counts and asymptotic classes answer different questions, so retain the concrete count before simplifying its growth.
''', 'Linear search checks indices 0 through n−1 until it finds target 8. Compare [8,1,2,3], [1,2,3,8] and [1,2,3,4], then derive worst-case equality comparisons for general n≥1.', r'Early success::C([8,1,2,3])=1::The first equality succeeds and terminates the search.~Late success or failure::C([1,2,3,8])=C([1,2,3,4])=4::Both inputs require checking every slot, although only one finds the target.~Generalize the maximum::C_{worst}(n)=n::No slot is checked twice, and an absent target makes all n checks necessary.~Give tight growth::C_{worst}(n)=\Theta(n)::The achievable lower bound and universal upper bound coincide.', 'Worst-case linear search uses exactly n equality comparisons, giving Θ(n) growth; the best case uses one.', 'For n=4 the general bound predicts four comparisons, attained by both the final-match and absent-target inputs. No distribution was assumed.')

T('4-search-sort', 'sorting-stability', 'Sorting', 'Stable sorting: trace insertion and equal-key order', r'''
Sorting orders records by a chosen key, while the rest of each record must remain attached to that key. A stable sort preserves the relative order of records whose keys compare equal. Sortedness alone says nothing about that relative order. Label equal-key records to distinguish them when tracing an algorithm, because replacing every record by its key can hide an unstable exchange.
Insertion sort maintains a sorted prefix. Take the next record, shift larger prefix records right and place the saved record into the gap. If the shift condition is strictly greater than the saved key, equal keys do not cross each other and the procedure is stable. Shifting on greater-than-or-equal can reverse equal-key records while still producing correctly ordered numeric keys.
Each insertion makes the sorted prefix one record longer. In the worst case, many prefix records move at each step, giving Θ(n²) time; an already sorted input can use linear comparisons in a standard implementation. Verify the ordering, that every original record appears exactly once, and the relative order of each equal-key group. Stability is a correctness requirement only when the task asks for it.
''', 'Stable insertion-sort records [2a,1b,2c,1d] by their numeric keys. Shift only records with a strictly larger key.', r'Insert one-b::::Save 1b, shift 2a right and obtain [1b,2a,2c,1d]. The prefix of length two is sorted.~Insert two-c::::The preceding record 2a has an equal key, so it is not shifted. The length-three prefix remains [1b,2a,2c].~Insert one-d::::Shift 2c and 2a, then stop at equal-key 1b. Insert 1d after 1b to obtain [1b,1d,2a,2c].', 'The sorted records are [1b,1d,2a,2c], preserving b before d and a before c.', 'Numeric keys are nondecreasing and every label remains. A greater-than-or-equal shift rule would move 1b past 1d and violate the required stability.')

T('4-search-sort', 'merge-sort-full-trace', 'Merge sort', 'Merge sort from recursive splits to a stable result', r'''
Merge sort recursively divides a sequence into smaller contiguous halves until each subproblem has at most one record. Those base subproblems are already sorted. It then merges sorted children while returning through the recursion tree. A merge repeatedly compares the two first unconsumed records and takes the smaller; once either side is empty, append the remaining side without further cross-side comparisons.
To preserve stability, take a left-side record before an equal-key right-side record. Contiguous division keeps original positions on each side, and applying that tie rule at every merge preserves original equal-key order through the whole algorithm. A trace of one isolated merge does not show the recursive sort; identify both the split tree and the sequence of combined subproblems.
Balanced division creates logarithmically many levels, and every level handles a linear total number of records, giving Θ(n log n) time. A conventional array implementation needs Θ(n) temporary merge storage in addition to logarithmic recursion depth. Exact comparison counts depend on how quickly one child is exhausted. Verify the final permutation and stability separately from its asymptotic bound.
''', 'Merge-sort [4a,1b,3c,1d], dividing into contiguous halves and choosing the left record on equal keys. Count cross-side comparisons.', r'Split to base cases::::Divide into [4a,1b] and [3c,1d], then into four one-record sequences.~Merge each pair::::One comparison makes [1b,4a] and one makes [1d,3c]. Each pair appends its remaining record after the comparison.~Merge the halves::::Compare 1b with 1d and take 1b on the tie; then take 1d, then 3c, and append 4a.~Count comparisons::1+1+3=5::The final append does not compare opposite sides because the right side is exhausted.', 'The stable sorted result is [1b,1d,3c,4a], using five cross-side comparisons.', 'All four records appear once and b precedes d as in the input. The two singleton merges and the final merge explicitly account for the complete recursive sort.')

T('4-search-sort', 'quicksort-lomuto', 'Quicksort', 'Quicksort: trace a stated Lomuto partition', r'''
Quicksort partitions a sequence around a pivot and recursively sorts the remaining regions. Exact partition traces require an explicit scheme: different schemes use different scan directions, equal-key rules and return meanings. Lomuto partition with a last-element pivot maintains a prefix whose values are at most the pivot, followed by inspected values greater than it, followed by uninspected values and the pivot at the end.
Start boundary i one position before the subarray. Scan j from the first position through the position before the pivot. Whenever A[j] is at most the pivot, increase i and swap A[i] with A[j]. Finally swap the pivot into i+1. The returned index is the pivot's final position, so recurse on regions excluding it. The pivot does not necessarily stay where it began.
Partition requires linear work for the current subarray. Recursion cost depends on the split sizes; balanced splits give Θ(n log n) while repeatedly extreme splits give Θ(n²). Equal values can cause poor splits for this two-way scheme. Check the invariant at every movement and use the returned boundary to ensure that recursive calls shrink.
''', 'Apply Lomuto partition to [4,2,5,1,3] with final pivot 3, zero-based indices and comparison A[j]≤pivot. Show the resulting recursive regions.', r'Initialize and inspect four::i=-1,\quad j=0::Since 4>3, no boundary move occurs; the small-value prefix stays empty.~Accept two::::At j=1, increase i to 0 and swap, giving [2,4,5,1,3]. At j=2, the value 5 is rejected.~Accept one::::At j=3, increase i to 1 and swap, giving [2,1,5,4,3]. The prefix [2,1] is at most the pivot.~Place the pivot::q=i+1=2::Swap positions 2 and 4 to obtain [2,1,3,4,5]. Recursively sort positions 0..1 and 3..4.', 'Partition produces [2,1,3,4,5] with pivot index 2; finishing the two recursive regions gives [1,2,3,4,5].', 'Both values left of the pivot are ≤3 and both values right are >3. All original values remain, and the left region shows why partitioning alone is not a complete sort.')

T('4-hashing', 'load-factor-resize', 'Load factor', 'Load factor: calculate occupancy and rehash after growth', r'''
A hash table's load factor is α=n/m, where n is the number of stored keys and m is the number of buckets or slots. It measures average occupancy, not the length of the longest collision cluster or chain. Two tables with the same load can have very different collision patterns. Chaining permits α greater than one because a bucket can hold several records; open addressing stores at most one live record per slot.
A resizing policy can check load against a chosen threshold before or after insertion. State that policy before deciding when growth occurs. Resizing changes the range of the hash function, so every stored key must be assigned to its new bucket or probe sequence. Merely expanding the old array and leaving records in their previous buckets can make lookups follow different locations and miss existing keys.
Expected chained lookup cost is O(1+α) under suitable distribution assumptions; the ratio alone does not prevent all keys from colliding. An individual resize costs linear work to move existing records, although appropriate geometric growth permits amortized bounds over many updates. Separate the current load calculation from distribution assumptions and from the cost of rebuilding.
''', 'A chained table has m=4 buckets, keys 1,5,9 and h(k)=k mod m. Insert 13; if the resulting load exceeds 0.75, double m and rehash all keys.', r'Calculate the current ratio::\alpha=3/4=0.75::The three keys share bucket one, but the load is based on total keys divided by total buckets.~Insert and trigger growth::\alpha=4/4=1>0.75::All four keys initially hash to bucket one, then the specified after-insertion policy doubles the capacity.~Rehash under eight buckets::1\bmod8=9\bmod8=1,\quad5\bmod8=13\bmod8=5::The new chains are bucket 1=[1,9] and bucket 5=[5,13].~Compute the new load::\alpha=4/8=0.5::Rebuilding changes bucket placement and lowers average occupancy.', 'After resizing there are eight buckets, load 0.5 and two chains of length two in buckets 1 and 5.', 'The new chains contain all four keys exactly once. Looking up 5 with modulus eight now selects bucket five, so leaving it in its old bucket one would be incorrect.')

T('4-greedy-divide', 'greedy-activity-selection', 'Greedy', 'Greedy activity selection by earliest finishing time', r'''
A greedy method commits to a locally chosen feasible action and continues on the remaining problem. For maximum-cardinality activity selection, the objective is the number of nonoverlapping activities, not total duration or profit. Treat intervals as half-open so an activity may start exactly when the previous one finishes. Sort by nondecreasing finishing time, accept the first activity, then accept each later activity whose start is at least the last accepted finish.
The earliest-finishing choice leaves the greatest opportunity for later activities. An exchange argument establishes that some optimal schedule begins with that choice: replace an optimal first activity by the one finishing no later, without invalidating its later activities. Apply the same reasoning after each selected finish. Examples illustrate the algorithm, while this argument justifies it for all legal instances.
During a trace, keep the sorted order and the current last finish. A rejected activity changes neither the schedule nor that finish. Choosing earliest start or shortest duration is a different rule and is not automatically correct. Sorting costs O(n log n), followed by a linear scan; presorted input needs only the scan.
''', 'Select a maximum number of compatible activities: A=(0,4), B=(1,2), C=(2,3), D=(3,5), E=(4,6), F=(5,7). Equal endpoints are compatible.', r'Sort by finish::::The order is B,C,A,D,E,F with finishing times 2,3,4,5,6,7.~Accept the early compatible pair::finish(B)=2,\quad start(C)=2\ge2::Choose B first; C starts at B\'s finish, so choose C and set last finish to 3.~Scan the middle choices::::Reject A because 0<3; choose D because 3≥3; reject E because 4<5.~Finish the schedule::5\ge5::Choose F. Rejections did not change the last accepted finish.', 'The greedy schedule is B,C,D,F, containing four nonoverlapping activities.', 'The intervals join at endpoints 2,3,5. Starting with the earliest-start activity A would permit at most two activities here (A,E or A,F); the safe criterion is earliest finish, justified by the exchange argument.')

T('4-greedy-divide', 'exchange-activity-proof', 'Exchange argument', 'Exchange an optimal first activity for the greedy one', r'''
An exchange argument proves a greedy choice is safe by transforming an optimal solution that may omit it into another optimal solution containing it. Identify the optimum's first choice, substitute the greedy choice and prove both feasibility and objective value are preserved. An example in which greedy succeeds cannot replace this general proof, and a counterexample to another rule does not establish the desired rule.
For maximum activity count, let g be an activity with minimum finish among eligible activities and let a be the first activity in an arbitrary optimal schedule. The inequality finish(g)≤finish(a) means every later scheduled activity, whose start is at least finish(a), also starts after g. Replacing a by g preserves nonoverlap and replaces one activity by one, leaving the count unchanged.
The remaining activities form the same kind of problem restricted to starts at least finish(g). If an optimal schedule's residual part were not optimal for that restriction, replacing it by a larger compatible residual schedule would improve the full schedule. Induction on the number of remaining activities now justifies repeated greedy choices. Specify endpoint and tie conventions; equality of finishes does not invalidate the inequality.
''', 'Activities are g=(1,3), a=(2,4), b=(4,5), c=(5,7), d=(7,9). Exchange the first activity of schedule [a,b,c,d] for g, then state the general optimality argument.', r'Compare first finishes::finish(g)=3\le4=finish(a)::g is earliest finishing, so it can only create more room after the first selection.~Check the numeric replacement::start(b)=4\ge3::The unchanged tail b,c,d remains compatible; [g,b,c,d] has the same four activities in count.~Establish the universal replacement::start(x)\ge finish(a)\ge finish(g)::For any later activity x in any optimum starting with a, the same inequalities preserve feasibility after replacement.~Apply the residual optimum argument::::A better schedule after finish(g) would improve the whole schedule. Repeating the exchange on that residual problem proves earliest-finish selection optimal.', 'The exchanged schedule [g,b,c,d] is feasible with count four. Every optimal schedule can be transformed to begin with an earliest-finishing activity without losing count.', 'The example demonstrates the exchange directly, and the arbitrary-optimum inequality supplies the proof beyond this instance. Because only g and a overlap here, four is also the maximum possible count.')

T('4-dynamic', 'optimal-substructure-knapsack', 'Optimal substructure', 'Optimal substructure: justify each knapsack subproblem', r'''
Optimal substructure means that an optimum for a defined state can be assembled from optimal answers to the correct smaller states. The state must retain all restrictions relevant to future decisions. For 0/1 knapsack, dp[i,w] represents the maximum value obtainable using only the first i items with capacity at most w. Both the item prefix and the remaining capacity matter; a capacity-only state without item-processing context can accidentally permit reuse.
Partition feasible solutions by their decision on item i. An excluding optimum must use the best solution for dp[i−1,w]. If it includes item i, its remaining chosen items must realize dp[i−1,w−weight(i)]; otherwise replacing them by a better valid subset would improve the supposed optimum. Add the included item's value and take the larger feasible branch.
This replacement reasoning proves the recurrence rather than merely recognizing a pattern in a few table values. It differs from overlapping subproblems, which explains why caching is useful. Base states use zero value for an empty item prefix under the at-most-capacity, nonnegative-value formulation. After evaluating a state, reconstruct the choices and verify capacity and one-use constraints.
''', 'Capacity is 5. The first two items are A=(weight 2,value 3), B=(3,4). Add C=(2,5). Justify and evaluate dp[3,5].', r'Exclude the final item::dp[2,5]=3+4=7::The best prior-prefix subset is A,B, filling capacity five.~Include the final item::5+dp[2,3]=5+4=9::After choosing C, the remaining capacity is three and B is the best feasible prior-prefix item.~Take the complete maximum::dp[3,5]=\max(7,9)=9::Every feasible subset either includes C or excludes it, so no third branch is missing.~Justify use of an optimal child::::If the included branch used A instead of the better fitting B, replacing A by B would increase value while retaining feasibility. Hence an optimum uses the optimal residual subset.', 'The optimal subset is B,C, with total weight 5 and total value 9.', 'All possibilities are covered by include/exclude. A,C has value 8 and weight 4; A,B,C has weight 7 and is infeasible. The reported subset uses each item once.')

T('4-dynamic', 'memoization-cache-trace', 'Memoization', 'Memoization: trace cache misses and repeated requests', r'''
Memoization wraps a recursive subproblem solver with a cache indexed by its state. On each request, first check whether the state is already stored. A hit returns that result without recomputing its dependencies; a miss evaluates the recurrence, stores the result and returns it. Cache keys must contain every input that changes the answer, and the cached object must remain valid for the current problem instance.
For Fibonacci, state k fully determines F(k), so repeated requests for the same k can reuse one value. A left-to-right recursive evaluation still creates requests for already cached states; those requests are not new computations. Distinguish the number of distinct solved states from total function invocations and cache accesses. Whether base states are prepopulated affects the first misses but not the values.
With O(1) cache lookup and constant work per transition, solving F(n) computes only O(n) distinct states instead of an exponential recursion tree. The first descent can still create O(n) active call frames, and the cache itself has O(n) entries. Memoization saves repeated work rather than eliminating every recursive call or making every recursion use constant memory.
''', 'Compute F(4) using F(k)=F(k−1)+F(k−2), evaluating the left child first. Start with cached F(0)=0 and F(1)=1; check the cache at every call.', r'Descend through misses::::Requests F(4), F(3) and F(2) miss, so their recursive evaluations remain suspended.~Solve the first missing state::F(2)=F(1)+F(0)=1::Both child requests hit prepopulated entries; store F(2)=1.~Resume three::F(3)=F(2)+F(1)=2::The returned F(2) result and another cache hit for F(1) complete the state; store it.~Resume four::F(4)=F(3)+F(2)=3::The second F(2) request hits instead of expanding again. Seven function requests include only three missing-state computations.', 'F(4)=3. The initial two base entries plus three newly computed states fill five cache entries; the evaluation makes seven requests.', 'The call requests in order are 4,3,2,1,0,1,2. Repeated 1 and 2 requests reuse results; the longest active chain is 4→3→2→1, four frames.')

T('4-graph-algorithms', 'traversal-components', 'Graph traversals', 'Graph traversal: reachability and a complete search forest', r'''
A graph traversal systematically discovers vertices through incident or outgoing edges. A search from one starting vertex covers only the vertices reachable from that start; it does not automatically visit an isolated vertex or another connected component. Maintain a visited set and mark a vertex when it is discovered, so cycles and multiple incoming routes do not repeatedly expand the same vertex.
For an undirected graph, a full traversal wraps BFS or DFS in an outer loop over all vertices. Whenever the next vertex is unvisited, start a new search and record a new component root. Discovery edges form a search forest: each nonroot discovered vertex has one parent, while already visited neighbors create no new forest edge. With c components and V vertices, the forest has V−c discovery edges.
An adjacency-list implementation checks every header and scans each neighbor entry, giving Θ(V+E) time for a full traversal. Neighbor order changes the exact discovery tree but not the component partition. Reachability, minimum spanning weight and weighted shortest paths are different tasks; simply visiting vertices does not optimize edge weights. Verify coverage explicitly when the input is disconnected.
''', 'Undirected graph vertices are A,B,C,D,E, edges A–B, B–C, D–E. Run a full BFS forest in vertex order A,B,C,D,E, using alphabetical neighbor order.', r'Start the first search::::A is unvisited, so mark it and begin a component. Expanding A discovers B; expanding B discovers C.~Finish the first component::::The queue empties after C, giving component {A,B,C}. The outer loop skips the now-visited B and C.~Start the next unvisited root::::D is unvisited, so a second search discovers E and completes component {D,E}.~Count the forest::V-c=5-2=3::Discovery edges are A–B, B–C and D–E, one per nonroot vertex.', 'The traversal order is A,B,C,D,E and the two components are {A,B,C} and {D,E}.', 'A search from A alone reaches only A,B,C. The outer loop adds exactly the missing D,E; every vertex is marked once and all three forest edges connect newly discovered vertices.')

T('4-bfs-dfs', 'dfs-discovery-finish', 'DFS', 'Depth-first search: discovery, returns and a back edge', r'''
Depth-first search marks a vertex when entering it, recursively explores each unvisited neighbor, then finishes the vertex after its adjacency scan returns. A recursive invocation suspends its parent's scan, so discovery order and finish order differ. With a fixed vertex and neighbor order, maintain white for unvisited, gray for active and black for finished vertices. An explicit stack can implement the same schedule if it preserves suspended scan positions.
For a directed graph, an edge to a gray vertex is a back edge to an active ancestor and proves a directed cycle. An edge to a black vertex does not establish the same fact. In an undirected graph, the immediate reverse edge to the parent needs separate handling before using an already visited neighbor as a cycle test.
Increment a timestamp on every discovery and every finish when that convention is specified. Ancestors' time intervals enclose descendants' intervals. Reverse finishing order is topological only when the directed graph has no cycle. Do not substitute a zero-indegree queue algorithm for a DFS trace: it can produce a valid topological order but has different active states, discovery edges and return events.
''', 'Run recursive DFS from A on directed lists A=[B,C], B=[D], C=[D], D=[B]. Use these neighbor orders, time initially zero and increment it on every discovery and finish.', r'Descend the first path::d(A)=1,\quad d(B)=2,\quad d(D)=3::A suspends while B explores; B suspends while D explores.~Recognize the cycle::::D sees B gray and active, so D→B is a back edge and B→D→B is a directed cycle.~Return and explore the remaining child::f(D)=4,\quad f(B)=5,\quad d(C)=6::Returning resumes A\'s scan at C. C sees D black and makes no recursive call.~Finish the remaining frames::f(C)=7,\quad f(A)=8::Each adjacency scan completes before its own finish event.', 'Discovery order is A,B,D,C; finish order is D,B,C,A. The back edge D→B proves the graph cyclic.', 'B\'s interval [2,5] encloses D\'s [3,4]. Reversing the finish order cannot be topological because the two directed edges between B and D demand incompatible orders.')

T('4-minimum-spanning', 'cut-exchange-proof', 'Cut property', 'Cut property: exchange a crossing edge into a tree', r'''
A cut partitions an undirected graph's vertices into two nonempty sides. An edge crosses the cut when exactly one endpoint is on each side. Every spanning tree must contain a crossing edge, otherwise its two sides would be disconnected. The cut property says a lightest crossing edge is contained in some minimum spanning tree; if it is uniquely lightest, every minimum spanning tree contains it.
To prove safety, take an MST omitting the candidate e and add e. The added edge creates exactly one cycle. Because that cycle crosses from one cut side to the other and eventually returns, it includes another crossing edge f. Remove f. The result is a spanning tree, and weight(e)≤weight(f) ensures its total does not increase. If the inequality is strict, an MST omitting e would contradict optimality.
The cut need not isolate one vertex. Compare only edges crossing the particular cut; a very cheap edge within one side is irrelevant to this application. Tied light edges support existence in some MST but not universal membership. Verify the chosen partition, crossing set and removed cycle edge before applying the theorem.
''', 'Graph edges are AB=1, AC=2, BC=3, BD=4, CD=5. Use cut {A,B}|{C,D} to justify AC, starting from spanning tree T={AB,BC,BD}.', r'Identify the crossing set::::AC, BC and BD cross this cut with weights 2,3,4. AB and CD lie within the two sides.~Select the unique minimum::2<3<4::AC is the uniquely lightest crossing edge, so every MST must include it.~Perform the exchange::::Add AC to T, creating cycle A–B–C–A. Remove crossing edge BC to recover a connected acyclic set.~Compare weights::1+2+4=7<1+3+4=8::The replacement reduces total weight while preserving all four vertices.', 'AC is mandatory in every MST. The exchange yields tree {AB,AC,BD}, lowering weight from 8 to 7.', 'The resulting three edges connect all four vertices without a cycle. The original T was deliberately not an MST; its improvement illustrates the contradiction that would arise if an MST omitted the uniquely lightest cut edge.')

T('4-minimum-spanning', 'disjoint-set-compression', 'Disjoint sets', 'Union–find: representatives and path compression', r'''
A disjoint-set structure partitions elements into nonoverlapping groups. Each group is represented by a rooted parent-pointer tree whose root points to itself. Find follows parent pointers to that root; equality of representatives establishes group membership, not equality of the original element labels. Union joins two different roots while leaving elements already in the same group unchanged.
Path compression changes each traversed parent to point directly to the root, preserving membership while shortening future searches. Union by rank or size independently limits growth when merging roots. Use the specified tie rule when exact parent arrays matter. Rank is a balancing estimate and need not equal actual height after compression; it must not be freely decremented whenever a path is shortened.
Parent links in union–find are bookkeeping links between representatives, rather than necessarily edges of the input graph. Kruskal uses finds to ask whether an edge would connect different selected-edge components, then unions those components after accepting it. A compressed parent tree can change dramatically without changing any selected graph edge. The efficient amortized bound applies to an operation sequence, not a claim that every uncompressed find takes one step.
''', 'Parent pointers are A→A, B→A, C→B, D→D. Run find(C) with full path compression, then union(C,D), attaching root D to root A.', r'Follow the old chain::C\to B\to A::A points to itself, so it is the representative of C\'s set.~Compress the path::::Set C.parent=A and keep B.parent=A. The elements A,B,C still belong to the same set.~Find both union roots::find(C)=A,\quad find(D)=D::The roots differ, so union is required.~Attach the specified root::D.parent\leftarrow A::Every element now has representative A; a later union(B,D) would do nothing.', 'The final parent pointers are A→A, B→A, C→A, D→A, forming one set.', 'Compression changed only the access path to a representative. The first find performed two parent-link traversals, while a subsequent find(C) needs only C→A.')

T('4-shortest-paths', 'bellman-ford-passes', 'Bellman Ford', 'Bellman–Ford: ordered relaxation passes with a negative edge', r'''
Bellman–Ford computes single-source shortest distances by repeatedly relaxing every directed edge. Initialize the source to zero and other vertices to infinity. For edge u→v, update d(v) when d(u) is finite and d(u)+weight(u,v) is smaller. An in-place pass can propagate several edges immediately if the supplied processing order permits it, so exact intermediate values depend on that order.
Without a reachable negative cycle, a shortest route can be chosen simple and therefore has at most V−1 edges. After V−1 full passes, all finite shortest distances are correct. A further improving pass proves that a negative cycle is reachable from the source. Negative edges alone are allowed; they do not by themselves create an undefined shortest distance. An entirely unreachable negative cycle does not trigger this source's improving-pass test.
Stop early if a full pass makes no changes, because all edge inequalities then already hold. Guard infinity arithmetic instead of adding a weight to an absent route. The standard time bound is O(VE); storing predecessor pointers can reconstruct routes when improvements occur. Verify a reported distance by summing a corresponding path's weights and checking that no edge can still relax.
''', 'Source S, vertices S,A,B,C. Process edges in this exact order on every pass: B→C=3, A→B=−2, S→A=4, S→C=10. Trace three passes and the extra-cycle check.', r'First pass::::B and A are initially unreachable, so their outgoing relaxations do nothing. S→A gives 4 and S→C gives 10: distances (0,4,∞,10).~Second pass::d(B)\leftarrow4-2=2::B→C still runs before B is improved. The pass ends with (0,4,2,10).~Third pass::d(C)\leftarrow2+3=5::The first edge now improves C, giving (0,4,2,5); no later edge helps.~Check one more full pass::::Every candidate equals or exceeds its stored destination distance, so no improvement occurs and no reachable negative cycle is detected.', 'Final distances are S=0, A=4, B=2, C=5. A shortest S-to-C route is S,A,B,C.', 'The route weight is 4−2+3=5, below the direct edge of weight 10. Three passes equal V−1; the unchanged extra pass confirms the negative edge did not produce a reachable negative cycle.')
